**Proof.** We prove
$$\sum_{j=0}^{n}\left(\binom{3n+2-j}{j}2^{j}-\binom{3n+1-j}{j-1}2^{j-1}\right)=2^{3n},$$
with the second term equal to $0$ when $j=0$.

**Step 1: Rewrite the summand via coefficient extraction.**

Since $\binom{m}{k}2^{k}=[x^{k}](1+2x)^{m}$, we have
$$\binom{3n+2-j}{j}2^{j}=[x^{j}](1+2x)^{3n+2-j},\qquad \binom{3n+1-j}{j-1}2^{j-1}=[x^{j-1}](1+2x)^{3n+1-j}.$$

Using $[x^{j}]f(x)=[x^{0}]\,f(x)/x^{j}$, the summand becomes
$$a_{j}=[x^{0}]\!\left(\frac{(1+2x)^{3n+2-j}}{x^{j}}-\frac{(1+2x)^{3n+1-j}}{x^{j-1}}\right)=[x^{0}]\,\frac{(1+2x)^{3n+1-j}}{x^{j}}\bigl((1+2x)-x\bigr)=[x^{0}]\,(1+x)\,\frac{(1+2x)^{3n+1}}{\bigl(x(1+2x)\bigr)^{j}}.$$

**Step 2: Sum the geometric series.**

Set $r=x(1+2x)$. Then
$$S=\sum_{j=0}^{n}a_{j}=[x^{0}]\,(1+x)(1+2x)^{3n+1}\sum_{j=0}^{n}r^{-j}=[x^{0}]\,\frac{(1+x)(1+2x)^{3n+1}}{r-1}\left(r-r^{-n}\right).$$

Now $r-1=x+2x^{2}-1=(2x-1)(1+x)$, so $\dfrac{1+x}{r-1}=\dfrac{1}{2x-1}$. Hence
$$S=[x^{0}]\,\frac{(1+2x)^{3n+1}\cdot r}{2x-1}-[x^{0}]\,\frac{(1+2x)^{3n+1}}{r^{n}(2x-1)}.$$

**Step 3: Evaluate the two terms.**

*First term:* $\dfrac{r}{2x-1}=\dfrac{x(1+2x)}{2x-1}$ has only non-negative powers of $x$ (since $\frac{1}{2x-1}=-\sum_{k\ge0}(2x)^{k}$ and multiplying by $x$ shifts to $k\ge1$). Thus $[x^{0}]$ of the product with $(1+2x)^{3n+1}$ is $0$.

*Second term:* Using $r^{n}=x^{n}(1+2x)^{n}$,
$$-[x^{0}]\,\frac{(1+2x)^{2n+1}}{x^{n}(2x-1)}=[x^{n}]\,\frac{(1+2x)^{2n+1}}{1-2x}.$$

So $S=[x^{n}]\,\dfrac{(1+2x)^{2n+1}}{1-2x}$.

**Step 4: Extract the coefficient.**

Expand $\dfrac{1}{1-2x}=\sum_{k\ge0}2^{k}x^{k}$ and $(1+2x)^{2n+1}=\sum_{m=0}^{2n+1}\binom{2n+1}{m}2^{m}x^{m}$. The Cauchy product gives
$$[x^{n}]\,\frac{(1+2x)^{2n+1}}{1-2x}=\sum_{m=0}^{n}\binom{2n+1}{m}2^{m}\cdot 2^{n-m}=2^{n}\sum_{m=0}^{n}\binom{2n+1}{m}.$$

**Step 5: Use the half-sum identity.**

By the symmetry $\binom{2n+1}{m}=\binom{2n+1}{2n+1-m}$, the $2n+2$ binomial coefficients split into two equal halves:
$$\sum_{m=0}^{n}\binom{2n+1}{m}=\sum_{m=n+1}^{2n+1}\binom{2n+1}{m}=\frac{1}{2}\cdot 2^{2n+1}=2^{2n}.$$

Therefore
$$S=2^{n}\cdot 2^{2n}=2^{3n}.\qquad\blacksquare$$

